\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The theta neuron on the circle}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{For #{I<0} the canonical equation in \ref{fnip-0092} has two
equilibria on the circle. The filled point is the node, where
#{x=\tan(\frac{\theta}{2})} is negative, and the open point is the saddle.
At #{I=0} the two equilibria meet. For #{I>0} every solution
continues around the circle, and the period is #{\frac{\pi}{\sqrt{I}}}.}

\texfig{\begin{tikzpicture}[line cap=round,font=\small]
  \useasboundingbox (-0.3,-0.55) rectangle (6.35,2.85);
  \draw (1.35,1.25) circle[radius=1];
  \draw[->] (2.35,1.25) -- (2.35,0.85);
  \draw[->] (1.35,2.25) -- (0.95,2.25);
  \draw[->] (0.55,0.55) -- (0.95,0.25);
  \fill ({1.35+cos(-70)},{1.25+sin(-70)}) circle[radius=1.15pt];
  \draw ({1.35+cos(70)},{1.25+sin(70)}) circle[radius=1.2pt];
  \node at (1.35,-0.15) {$I<0$};
  \draw (4.55,1.25) circle[radius=1];
  \draw[->] (5.55,1.25) -- (5.55,1.65);
  \draw[->] (4.55,2.25) -- (4.15,2.25);
  \draw[->] (3.55,1.25) -- (3.55,0.85);
  \draw[->] (4.55,0.25) -- (4.95,0.25);
  \node at (4.55,-0.15) {$I>0$};
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{On the left, both branches of the flow run from the saddle to the
node. On the right the flow has one direction around the circle.
\citet{Exercise 8, p. 73}{ermentrout2010mathematical} asks for
these three phase lines, including the meeting at #{I=0}.}
